//? Title: Stochastic Gradients: Make the Estimator Match the Loss
//? Subtitle: SGD, SVRG and SAGA with exact component checks and Iris least squares
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A dataset loss is a sum of observation losses, but sampling one observation
usually estimates the gradient of their mean. Mixing those conventions changes
the update. We derive the estimators implemented by SGD, SVRG and SAGA, check a
two-row problem exactly, and compare their callback costs on generated regression
and Iris. The examples include the corrected mean-snapshot scaling in SVRG and
report finite-budget error rather than treating every normal return as convergence.
)
\toc

> The problem

A full gradient visits every row. With n observations and p coefficients, a dense
least-squares gradient costs O(n*p), whereas one row gradient costs O(p). Cheap
updates introduce sampling noise: even at the best fit, an individual row can
still have a nonzero residual. Variance reduction uses previously computed
information to cancel that noise near a solution.

> The method

Our fixture exposes the *sum* \(f(w)=\sum_i\ell_i(w)\), with
\(\ell_i(w)=\tfrac12(a_i^\top w-y_i)^2\) and \(g_i(w)=a_i(a_i^\top w-y_i)\).
The three native solvers update at the *mean* loss scale \(F=f/n\). Their returned
objective is still f. A learning rate for sum-gradient descent would be n times
smaller for the same displacement. Replicating rows changes the full-sum value
but leaves its unpenalised minimizer and the mean gradient unchanged.

SGD averages the gradients in each mini-batch and optionally reshuffles every
row once per epoch. It is sampling without replacement within an epoch, so its
conditional estimator is not the independent uniform draw assumed in the simplest
SGD proof. Here SVRG and SAGA sample a uniform row with replacement.

For SVRG, freeze a snapshot \(s\) for an epoch and compute \(G=\nabla f(s)/n\):

$$
 v_i(w,s)=g_i(w)-g_i(s)+G,\qquad
 \mathbb E_i[v_i(w,s)]=\nabla F(w),\qquad w^+=w-\eta v_i(w,s).
$$
\alttext(SVRG subtracts the sampled snapshot gradient and adds the mean full snapshot gradient. Its expected direction is the gradient of the mean component loss.)

The division by n is essential. Earlier native code added the full *sum* gradient
without dividing; that mixes scales and is biased away from the snapshot. This
conversion corrects that implementation. The estimator is deterministic when
w=s, but can fluctuate inside the epoch. This implementation retains the last
inner iterate as its next snapshot, not a randomly selected inner iterate.

The retained TikZ diagram needs LaTeX/TikZ, amsmath, lmodern and dvisvgm to rebuild.
Normal documentation exports use its saved local SVG.

```{bash, file="figures/opt_variance_mechanism.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/opt_variance_mechanism.tikz > build/opt_variance_mechanism.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/opt_variance_mechanism.svg build/opt_variance_mechanism.dvi > build/opt_variance_mechanism.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered the optimization mechanism diagram.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered the optimization mechanism diagram.
// \image(figures/opt_variance_mechanism.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A snapshot control variate changes variance without changing the mean-loss direction.)
\alttext(Subtract the same row gradient at the snapshot, then add the mean snapshot gradient. The result has the correct mean direction while retaining explicit callback costs.)

SAGA stores one gradient t_i for every row and their mean tbar. Its direction is
g_i(w)-t_i+tbar; afterwards it replaces t_i by g_i(w) and updates tbar. Native
storage is O(n*p), compared with O(p) for SGD and SVRG, excluding the dataset.
The optional trace adds O(number of callbacks*p) storage for all three.
The native SAGA implementation here is smooth and has no composite proximal step.

SGD and SVRG stop on an absolute full-sum loss change at an epoch boundary.
SAGA also tests the last sampled estimator norm there. A small change or one
small estimator need not imply a small full gradient, especially with noisy,
constant-rate SGD. All stop tests should be read with the final diagnostic.

> Small worked example

Take \(a=(1,2)\), \(y=(1,3)\) and one coefficient \(w\). Then \(g_0=w-1\),
\(g_1=4w-6\), \(\nabla F(w)=2.5w-3.5\), the optimum is \(w=1.4\) and \(f=0.1\).
At snapshot zero and current w=one, the two corrected SVRG directions are
-2.5 and .5. Their mean -one agrees with gradient(F)(one). Omitting the
normalisation produces mean -4.5 instead. At the snapshot both directions
are -3.5. With eta=.1 the first step is therefore .35 regardless of the row;
the second is .665 if row zero is selected and .56 if row one is selected.
The table records the actual sampled component indices, including the paired
current/snapshot requests, instead of assuming a cross-library random transcript.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_variance_two_rows.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o = dm::optim;
  o::LeastSquaresObjective f({{1}, {2}}, {1, 3});
  o::SVRGOptions           c;
  c.step_size           = .1;
  c.max_epochs          = 1;
  c.inner_iterations    = 2;
  c.tolerance           = 0;
  const auto run        = o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableSeparableFunction>(f, o::SVRG(c), {0});
  std::vector<double> w = dm::seql(-.1, 1.7, 201).values(), loss;
  for (double x : w)
    loss.push_back(f.evaluate({x}));
  const auto req = run.trace.sample(f, "term_gradient");
  auto       p   = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
               .line(w, loss, "sum loss", {130, 130, 130})
               .points(
                   req.numeric_column("x0").values(),
                   req.numeric_column("value").values(),
                   "row-gradient requests",
                   {40, 100, 190},
                   4)
               .title("SVRG: inspect the paired requests")
               .x_label("coefficient w")
               .y_label("full sum loss")
               .x_limits(-.1, 1.7)
               .y_limits(0, 9)
               .legend({"sum loss", "row-gradient requests"}, {{130, 130, 130}, {40, 100, 190}});
  p.size(850, 490).save_svg("build/opt_variance_two_rows.svg");
  std::print(
      "{}\n\n{}\n\nMean-loss L={:.1f}; f(1.4)={:.1f}.\n",
      req.to_md(8),
      run.summary().to_md(8),
      f.mean_loss_lipschitz(),
      f.evaluate({1.4}));
}
```
