> Checking the theory

>> An exactly fitted generated regression

Use four design rows (1,0), (0,1), (1,1), (2,-1), response (2,-3,-1,7),
start (0,0) and true coefficient (2,-3). Every row gradient is zero at this
solution, so ordinary SGD can also lose its sampling noise here. All methods
use eta=.02 and seed 42, with tolerance zero: SGD has 300 epochs, SVRG 100
epochs of four inner steps, and SAGA 1200 updates. These are different costs.
The horizontal axis counts one for a row gradient and n for a full gradient;
SVRG uses two row gradients per inner step. Objective calls and trace replay
are additional work, so this axis is not wall time or an overall speed ranking.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_variance_exact_fit.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o = dm::optim;
  o::LeastSquaresObjective f({{1, 0}, {0, 1}, {1, 1}, {2, -1}}, {2, -3, -1, 7});
  o::SGDOptions            a;
  a.step_size  = .02;
  a.max_epochs = 300;
  a.tolerance  = 0;
  o::SVRGOptions b;
  b.step_size  = .02;
  b.max_epochs = 100;
  b.tolerance  = 0;
  o::SAGAOptions c;
  c.step_size      = .02;
  c.max_iterations = 1200;
  c.tolerance      = 0;
  std::vector<o::OptimizationRun> runs{
      o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableSeparableFunction>(f, o::SGD(a), {0, 0}),
      o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableSeparableFunction>(f, o::SVRG(b), {0, 0}),
      o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableSeparableFunction>(f, o::SAGA(c), {0, 0})};
  const std::vector<std::string> names{"SGD", "SVRG", "SAGA"};
  o::optimization_comparison(f, runs, names, "value", 0, false, true)
      .title("Exact-fit control: different declared budgets")
      .size(850, 490)
      .save_svg("build/opt_variance_exact_fit.svg");
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < runs.size(); ++i)
    std::print(
        "{}\n\n{}\n\n{}\n\n",
        names[i],
        runs[i].summary().to_md(8),
        runs[i].trace.counts().to_md(0));
}
```
