\caption(The direct least-squares solution anchors the loss reference, while full gradient norms expose finite-budget error.)
\alttext(SGD, SVRG and SAGA curves use the same raw-unit Iris design and learning rate but different declared budgets. Callback counts include the gradient-table and snapshot costs.)

>> Learning-rate sensitivity is still present

Repeat the same raw Iris design with rates .001, .01 and .03. Each run is SVRG
with 100 epochs and 150 inner steps, zero tolerance and seed 42. A control variate
does not make an arbitrary step stable. Recorded nonfinite requests and native
exceptions are retained with partial coordinates; a finite returned value also
needs a full-gradient check. A stable rate can still be too slow in small-curvature
directions. These three rates illustrate sensitivity, without estimating a
sampling distribution or claiming universal convergence guarantees.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_variance_rates.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o                 = dm::optim;
  const auto          iris    = dm::datasets::iris();
  const std::string   formula = "Petal.Length ~ Sepal.Length + Sepal.Width + Petal.Width";
  auto                f       = o::LeastSquaresObjective::from_data(iris, formula);
  const dm::stats::LM ref(iris, formula);
  std::vector<o::OptimizationRun> runs;
  std::vector<std::string>        names;
  for (double eta : {.001, .01, .03}) {
    o::SVRGOptions c;
    c.step_size  = eta;
    c.max_epochs = 100;
    c.tolerance  = 0;
    runs.push_back(
        o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableSeparableFunction>(
            f, o::SVRG(c), std::vector<double>(4, 0)));
    names.push_back(std::format("eta={}", eta));
    std::print("{}\n\n{}\n\n", names.back(), runs.back().summary().to_md(8));
  }
  o::optimization_comparison(f, runs, names, "value", ref.rss() / 2, false, true)
      .title("SVRG still depends on its learning rate")
      .size(850, 490)
      .save_svg("build/opt_variance_rates.svg");
}
```
